WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:03.100
ZEDD: I think every little
detail you add into a song

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.266
is part of the reason
why you like it so much.

00:00:06.266 --> 00:00:08.900
Hey, Anton, what's up, man?

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.066
Good, how are you doing?

00:00:10.066 --> 00:00:11.233
Hey, welcome to
"One Strange Rock."

00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:12.700
Thank you.

00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.133
There's some really crazy
footage I want you to see.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:14.900
Come on.
Check it out.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:15.667
Let's do it.

00:00:15.667 --> 00:00:18.333
[music playing]

00:00:26.767 --> 00:00:32.266
I am going to tell you about
the most incredible place.

00:00:32.266 --> 00:00:33.300
And you know what?

00:00:38.066 --> 00:00:39.900
You're walking on it.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:42.633
You know, so when you're
looking at those images,

00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:45.266
I had a chance to see
some of that from space.

00:00:45.266 --> 00:00:48.233
And as you go around the
planet, every 90 minutes,

00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:51.333
you see a sunrise and a
sunset every 45 minutes.

00:00:51.333 --> 00:00:54.400
And you're flying over places
like Afghanistan and Iraq,

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:56.233
places where there's
fighting and wars,

00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.767
but you don't see any
of that from space.

00:00:57.767 --> 00:00:59.867
You just see beauty.

00:00:59.867 --> 00:01:02.867
So I've always been a huge
fan of Darren Aronofsky.

00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:06.333
I got really excited when I
heard that he was doing this.

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:09.066
What was your experience
working with him?

00:01:09.066 --> 00:01:11.567
What he's putting together
now with "One Strange Rock"

00:01:11.567 --> 00:01:15.367
is just this visual
palette with all

00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:18.533
these different shapes and
colors and sounds and things.

00:01:18.533 --> 00:01:20.633
But it's like it takes
you to a whole other level

00:01:20.633 --> 00:01:22.967
about learning what
about your planet.

00:01:22.967 --> 00:01:24.500
He's brilliant.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:28.834
So you saw the visual spectrum
of this incredible footage

00:01:28.834 --> 00:01:30.133
and planet.

00:01:30.133 --> 00:01:31.767
I can't wait to hear
the acoustic spectrum

00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:33.266
that you're going to be--
- I can't wait.

00:01:33.266 --> 00:01:34.600
--bringing out.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.200
Well, it's really
interesting, because it's

00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:39.967
a lot of little things
that make a grand picture.

00:01:39.967 --> 00:01:41.300
Right.

00:01:41.300 --> 00:01:44.200
Do you sit down in one
take and write the song?

00:01:44.200 --> 00:01:45.633
It's a very long process.

00:01:45.633 --> 00:01:48.967
I like to start on the
piano with the harmony

00:01:48.967 --> 00:01:50.033
and the melody.

00:01:50.033 --> 00:01:52.033
That's the base.

00:01:52.033 --> 00:01:56.367
And I record it all in MIDI
and bring it into my computer.

00:01:56.367 --> 00:01:58.367
And there's several
stages of adding

00:01:58.367 --> 00:02:03.567
little bits and pieces, samples,
drums, shakers, experiment.

00:02:03.567 --> 00:02:06.633
You go through 200 kicks,
1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7,

00:02:06.633 --> 00:02:08.400
until you hear that one
that you think is right.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.033
And then you manipulate that.

00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:12.867
And you slowly build
your song structure,

00:02:12.867 --> 00:02:14.133
and you mix it all together.

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:17.133
And then you bounce
out one little file,

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:18.900
and that's what
people listen to.

00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:24.133
[music playing]

